{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: the Performance evidence credential repair at exact head 065d16fd17d7c2c4781eb6a03769892f3655ca1d (request/performance-evidence-credential, one commit on main c3169d83, pushed; exactly two files, internal/perfscenario/run.go and the new run_test.go; Rests-On governing request 15277 bb79f348; artifact 6e088c6d, statement 15323, rests on promise 51d93563 and is fresh). Review against request 15324; not merged. The request text cites the artifact as #15321; the governing artifact for this head is 6e088c6d at statement 15323.\n\nEvery named condition is met and independently reproduced on a detached checkout. openPerformanceCredential (run.go:733) posts exactly actor and ttl_ms with no client-chosen credential or session field, which on the server (internal/service/server.go:503) is the OpenTrustedSession mint path; it decodes the response with DisallowUnknownFields into the same two top-level fields as service.presenceResponse, credential and a real nexus.Change, rejects a trailing JSON value and an empty credential; request() already fails on any non-2xx. Enumerating every request the two scenarios make, /v0/act is the only authenticated one and both submit_wait (run.go:569) and concurrent_read_write (run.go:637) pass the minted credential to it; /v0/status, /v0/status-summary and /v0/wait need none. TestSessionBoundScenarioSetupUsesResidentCredential (run_test.go:12) drives prepareOperation for both scenarios through service.Server.Handler, the real strict decoder, ungated by GITSEQ_PERF_INTEGRATION, so CI runs it. Red proofs: reinstating the obsolete session field fails both subtests with \"json: unknown field session\"; sending a client-chosen credential fails both with \"credential is not valid\"; inventing the credential after minting fails each scenario with \"credential is not valid\"; dropping the credential from a later act fails with \"credential is required\". Smoke: the scheduled lane (performance.yml, tier smoke, depth 100) reproduced at real depth with the gitseq-perf binary built from this head passes all three contract concurrencies for concurrent_read_write and submit_wait, and the same fixture with a binary from c3169d83 fails both scenarios with the unknown-field error, so the defect and the fix are both demonstrated. Gates: gofmt, go build, go vet, git diff --check clean; go test ./internal/perfscenario -count=1 -race, ./internal/service/..., ./cmd/gitseq-perf/... and ./internal/perflane/... ok; the integration fixture test passes all nine scenarios. internal/service, host/live and both workflows are byte-identical to main.\n\nRecorded, not blocking. Three defensive guards in openPerformanceCredential have no red proof: removing DisallowUnknownFields, the empty-credential check, or the trailing-value check each leaves the package green, because the real server never emits those shapes; the comment at run.go:731-732 claims they make drift fail the normal suite, and a fifteen-line unit test driving the helper with an http.HandlerFunc stub returning an extra field, a missing credential and two concatenated objects would back it. The trailing-value block (run.go:750-755) is decoder.More() in one line and would drop the io import. The handler-with-telemetry block is repeated in three prepare functions plus prepareOperation, pre-existing and rightly left alone. For whoever merges: a live stale artifact bb3d410a at internal/perfscenario (commit 70f4ab0a, an ancestor of main) is the in-target predecessor to retire with 6e088c6d as its successor.\n\nArchitecture: internal/perfscenario is measurement tooling consuming layers six and seven, not a layer in docs/reference/architecture.md; no contract changes, and the harness now complies with the documented live-credential rule (clients never choose a credential). docs/reference/performance.md says nothing about presence or credentials, so nothing is falsified.\n\nSecurity: the credential is never printed, logged or written; response bodies reach the evidence directory only as a byte count; only Credential is used and only after a non-empty check; the trusted mint path is appropriate for a harness that owns its server and a throwaway fixture; failure modes are typed errors that exit the worker non-zero. The change narrows what the harness may do. No defect.\n\nSimplification: decoder.More() for the trailing-value block; otherwise the change is minimal and correctly does not widen into the pre-existing handler duplication.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:6e088c6df49336e6df8c2e045c0bc3c0a3448af5","head":"065d16fd17d7c2c4781eb6a03769892f3655ca1d","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:a018aeec933c76ffbf7b3d284a26c6d74fa7d9fb\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:fbd55564c69a36d3b6d236f9538ede4bfb05a4c8","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}